<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Skradin</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Scardona</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Skradin is a small town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, on the Krka river near Šibenik. As Scardona it was a Liburnian settlement and, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, the seat of the conventus, or judicial assembly, of the Liburnians. It later had a medieval bishopric and is now the main gateway to the Krka National Park.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/skradin.jpg" alt="Skradin" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jozef Kotulič · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Krka13Chorvatsko9.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Skradin lies near the Krka river and at the entrance to the Krka National Park, 17 kilometres from Šibenik and 100 kilometres from Split, in Šibenik-Knin County. The park's main attraction is the series of waterfalls called Slapovi Krke, the biggest of which, Skradinski buk, is named after the town. The municipality had a population of 3,349 at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and the town itself 508.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In antiquity the town was known as Scardon and Scardona, a name found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> and Ptolemy. Its ending is thought to be an Illyrian suffix, and the prevailing theory links the root of the name to a term meaning steep. After the Roman conquest Skradin became an administrative and military centre of the region and was mentioned as a municipium in 530. It was destroyed in the Migration Period in the 7th century and restored under Croatian rulers in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. In the 10th century it was one of the fortified towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, the centre of the Skradin županija.</p><p>Skradin flourished in the late 13th and early 14th centuries as the capital of the Šubić bans Paul I and Mladen II. They built the Turina fortress on the hill above the harbour, raised the settlement below it to a free city with its own statute and administration, and founded richly endowed monasteries that housed Dominicans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> and other orders.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> conquered the town in 1522 and held it, with some interruptions, until 1684. The Ottoman-Venetian wars devastated it and it was relocated. In October 1683 uskoks from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetian-dalmatia">Venetian Dalmatia</a>, mainly Morlachs from Ravni Kotari, took Skradin and other border towns whose Muslim population had fled, and the Venetians finally took it in 1684. It became the centre of a municipality in 1705, and was later occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> and then by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>. The centre of the region shifted to Šibenik, and the Diocese of Skradin was abandoned in 1828.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Skradin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They lived in fortified hill settlements grouped around centres such as Iader (Zadar), Aenona (Nin) and <b>Scardona</b>, with no single king recorded.</blockquote><a href="/state/liburnians">Liburnians</a></li></ul></section>
